WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.220
Housing crisis by city.

00:00:02.170 --> 00:00:06.579
The national average of eight times income masks extreme variation.

00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:09.431
Toronto: twelve times income.

00:00:09.431 --> 00:00:12.102
Vancouver: thirteen times.

00:00:12.102 --> 00:00:15.636
Montreal: seven times and rising rapidly.

00:00:15.636 --> 00:00:21.693
Median rent exceeds twenty-six hundred dollars per month in Toronto and twenty-eight hundred in Vancouver.

00:00:21.693 --> 00:00:29.715
These are the cities where immigrants concentrate, where jobs are located, and where the housing financialization documented on this site hits hardest.

00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:38.352
The city-specific data shows that the housing crisis is not abstract — it determines whether millions of Canadians can afford shelter in the cities where they work.

00:00:38.352 --> 00:00:41.704
City-by-city housing affordability comparison.
